<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="host-install"
      updated="September 3, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"install", "The install command"},
        {"confirm", "Confirm it connected"},
        {"config", "The config file"},
        {"flags", "Flags and environment"},
        {"user", "The runner's user and privileged actions"},
        {"next", "After it is connected"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="host-install">
        <:dek>
          The
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
            quickstart
          </.link>
          gets one runner online in a minute. This page has the rest: the install flags, the
          config file, and the privileges you grant.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For a container instead of a host, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/containers"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Install in a container</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          A Linux host with <strong class="text-zinc-300">systemd</strong>, such as a VM, cloud
          instance, or bare-metal host. macOS with launchd works for development and evaluation.
        </:item>
        <:item><code class="text-zinc-300">sudo</code> on that host.</:item>
        <:item>
          GitHub CLI with <.docs_inline_code>gh attestation verify --bundle</.docs_inline_code>. A GitHub login is
          not required.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          Outbound HTTPS to <code class="text-zinc-300">emisar.dev:443</code>
          for the control plane, installer, and release files, plus
          <code class="text-zinc-300">registry.emisar.dev:443</code>
          for action packs, <code class="text-zinc-300">tuf-repo-cdn.sigstore.dev:443</code>, and
          <code class="text-zinc-300">tuf-repo.github.com:443</code>
          for release verification. See
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/network-requirements"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Network requirements</.link>
          for the required public trust-root hosts and optional GitHub fallback domains.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.agent_skill_cta
        class="mt-8"
        skill="install-emisar"
        prompt="Install the emisar runner and verify it connects. First ask me which host to install it on and how you can reach it (local shell, SSH, or something else). My enrollment key: emkey-enroll-… (I can mint one at Connect a runner in the emisar console). Use the install-emisar skill: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AndrewDryga/emisar/main/skills/install-emisar/SKILL.md"
      >
        The <code class="text-zinc-300">install-emisar</code>
        skill walks your agent through this whole page: install, verify, evidence report.
      </.agent_skill_cta>

      <.docs_h2 id="install">The install command</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        In the console, click <a
          href={~p"/app/runners/install"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners → Connect a runner</a>. It shows this command with a fresh single-use key filled in. Run it as root on the host:
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="shell"
        copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… bash"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh \
    | sudo EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… bash</.docs_code>
      <.docs_verify_download
        tarball="emisar-<version>-linux-amd64.tar.gz"
        checksums="SHA256SUMS"
        bundle="SHA256SUMS.sigstore.jsonl"
        tag="runner-v<version>"
        workflow="AndrewDryga/emisar/.github/workflows/runner-release-trusted.yml"
      />
      <.docs_install_script_details />

      <.docs_h2 id="confirm">Confirm it connected</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner connects out as soon as its service starts. In the console, open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>
        — the host appears online, with the actions it advertises. On the host, run:
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/runner-fleet.webp"
        preview_h="max-h-[32rem]"
        title="emisar console · Runners"
        caption="A connected host shows a green connected badge with its last heartbeat; offline hosts stay listed with when they were last seen."
        alt="The Runners page grouped by runner group: api-iad-02, pg-primary-iad, and edge-fra-01 show green connected badges with heartbeats seconds ago, while edge-sfo-03 shows an amber offline badge last seen three hours ago."
      />
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell" copy_text="sudo emisar doctor"><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar doctor</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        It checks the config, credential, packs, binaries, and service in one pass. The install
        finished but the host never appears —
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet" <> "#troubleshooting"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Troubleshooting</.link>
        starts from that symptom.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="config">The config file</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.docs_inline_code>/etc/emisar/config.yaml</.docs_inline_code>
        is the configuration file, and you can edit it. It does not contain secrets — they live
        in <.docs_inline_code>runner.env</.docs_inline_code>. The fields
        you will set:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="/etc/emisar/config.yaml"><span class="text-sky-300">schema_version</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">1</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">runner</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">group</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"web"</span>              <span class="text-zinc-600"># the fleet tier this host belongs to (targeted by runbooks + scopes)</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">labels</span>:                    <span class="text-zinc-600"># free-form, for finer targeting</span>
    <span class="text-sky-300">role</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"frontend"</span>
    <span class="text-sky-300">region</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"us-east-1"</span>
  <span class="text-zinc-600"># id: "web-01"           # optional — the runner's declared name + identity; defaults to the hostname</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">cloud</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">url</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"https://emisar.dev"</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">enrollment_key_env</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY"</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">execution</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">inherit_env</span>:              <span class="text-zinc-600"># env-var names a pack can read for credentials</span>
    - <span class="text-brand-300">NOMAD_ADDR</span>
    - <span class="text-brand-300">NOMAD_TOKEN</span>
<span class="text-zinc-600"># admission:               # optional local defense-in-depth — see the security model</span>
<span class="text-zinc-600"># signing:                 # optional signed-dispatch enforcement — see signed dispatch</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Restart the service after editing (<.docs_inline_code>sudo systemctl restart emisar</.docs_inline_code>,
        or SIGHUP to reload packs without dropping runs).
        <.docs_inline_code>admission</.docs_inline_code>
        sets a local allowlist, denylist, and risk ceiling that the control plane cannot override
        — see the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security model</.link>.
        <.docs_inline_code>signing</.docs_inline_code>
        makes the runner refuse any dispatch not signed with your key — see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >signed dispatch</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="flags">Flags and environment</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Flags go after <.docs_inline_code>bash -s --</.docs_inline_code>; environment variables go before <.docs_inline_code>bash</.docs_inline_code>. The common options are below. For the complete installer argument list, see
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-cli#lifecycle"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Install, update, and removal</.link>
        in the Runner CLI reference.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-900">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[560px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/80 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-zinc-500">
              <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium">Flag / variable</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium">What it does</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-400">
            <%= for {name, meaning} <- [
              {"EMISAR_URL", "The control plane to enroll against, baked into config.yaml. Defaults to https://emisar.dev; set it only for a test or evaluation portal."},
              {"EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY", "The emkey-enroll-… key the runner exchanges for its own token on first connect."},
              {"EMISAR_GROUP", "How the console groups runners, written to runner.group. Defaults to the host's short hostname."},
              {"EMISAR_RUNNER_ID", "Registers the runner under a declared name and identity, written to runner.id. Defaults to the hostname — declare one for containers and hosts with colliding hostnames."},
              {"EMISAR_RUNNER_LABEL_<KEY>", "Bake a free-form runner label into config.yaml. For example, use EMISAR_RUNNER_LABEL_ROLE=web or EMISAR_RUNNER_LABEL_ENVIRONMENT=prod. The console filters and searches these labels."},
              {"--packs LIST", "Install named packs during setup. For example, use --packs linux-core,docker."},
              {"-y / --yes", "Skip the confirmation prompts for unattended installs. Requires an explicit --packs LIST or EMISAR_PACKS (an empty value installs none)."},
              {"--version TAG", "Pin a specific release instead of latest."},
              {"--no-start", "Install everything but do not start the service yet — for image bakes."},
              {"--no-service", "Binary only: skip the service user, unit, and activation. For containers and CI — see Containers."}
            ] do %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-mono text-xs text-brand-300">{name}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{meaning}</td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Run
        <.docs_inline_code>install.sh --help</.docs_inline_code>
        for path overrides and a custom service user. Set
        <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_GITHUB_TOKEN</.docs_inline_code>
        only if you use the optional GitHub release fallback and hit its rate limits.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="user">The runner's user and privileged actions</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The service runs as its own
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar</.docs_inline_code>
        user, not root, and every action runs as that user. By default an action can do what any
        user on the host can: read logs, list processes, check service status. The runner also
        blocks every action from gaining privileges through
        <.docs_inline_code>sudo</.docs_inline_code>
        or any other setuid program — a sudoers grant for the
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar</.docs_inline_code>
        user does nothing. When a pack action needs root, there are two ways in.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For systemd operations, grant exactly the operation with a polkit rule — systemd asks
        polkit who may manage its units, and no privilege changes hands in the action:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="/etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-emisar.rules"><span class="text-zinc-500">// let the emisar user restart exactly nginx, nothing else</span>
polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) &#123;
  if (subject.user == "emisar" &&
      action.id == "org.freedesktop.systemd1.manage-units" &&
      action.lookup("unit") == "nginx.service" &&
      action.lookup("verb") == "restart") &#123;
    return polkit.Result.YES;
  &#125;
&#125;);</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The action then calls
        <.docs_inline_code>systemctl restart nginx</.docs_inline_code>
        and polkit allows exactly that call. For packs that need more than systemd verbs, run the
        service as root instead and let policy, approvals, and the local admission list bound what
        runs. An action that declares a
        <.docs_inline_code>user:</.docs_inline_code>
        drops to that user before it runs, even when the runner account is broader. The rest of
        the host protections — the setuid block, the user drop, local admission, systemd hardening
        — are in the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security model</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="next">After it is connected</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Manage the runner fleet
        </.link>
        covers work after the first connection. It explains groups, labels, pack credentials,
        updates, reconnects, the host CLI, and removal. To boot ephemeral hosts from an autoscaling
        group with one reusable key, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/autoscaling-fleets"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >autoscaling fleets</.link>. To roll out across a fleet in phases, see the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/production"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Go to production</.link>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
